Before someone calls your company, visits your website, or meets your team, there's a good chance they've already asked AI about you.

What's the best firm for this? Who should I hire? Is this company any good?

And AI answers. Confidently. In full sentences. Whether or not it has the story right.

That means your first impression is no longer just your website or your sales team. It's whatever AI pieced together from everything it could find about you.

So here's the question I keep coming back to: if AI described your company to a stranger today, would it get you right?

## There's no default answer anymore

[Zen Media](https://zenmedia.com/) recently published a study on how AI talks about hotels and resorts. They asked ChatGPT, Gemini and Grok 1,000 traveler questions and read 3,015 answers.

Across those answers, AI named 5,242 different companies. Most of them, 3,581, showed up only once.

And the company with the biggest share? Expedia, with just 3.1% of all mentions. Marriott came in at 2.8%. Hilton at 2.6%.

Think about that. Some of the most recognizable names in travel show up in a tiny slice of the answers.

That tells me something important. AI doesn't hand out one standard answer. It builds a different picture depending on who's asking, what they want, and what it can find. A family planning a beach trip and a couple looking for a spa weekend get very different results.

One note worth sharing: Zen Media sells AI visibility services, so read the study with that in mind. But the pattern matches what many of us are seeing. Big brand recognition doesn't guarantee you'll show up, or show up accurately.

## This is a clarity problem, not an SEO problem

When marketers hear this, the first instinct is to treat it like search. More keywords. More pages. More optimization.

I understand the instinct. But I think it misses the real issue.

AI learns about you from everything that's out there. Your website, yes. But also your reviews, directory listings, old bios, press mentions, team profiles and articles you didn't write. If those pieces tell different stories, AI has to guess which one is true.

If your story doesn't line up, AI won't line it up for you.

So before chasing AI visibility, I'd start with a simpler question: could a stranger, or a machine, describe what you do in one clear sentence?

If the answer is no, no tactic will fix it. If the answer is yes, everything else gets easier.

I wrote about a version of this in [AI Isn't the Problem. Clarity Is.](https://socialmagicwithjen.com/blog/ai-isnt-the-problem-clarity-is/) That post was about getting AI to write like you. This one is about getting AI to describe you accurately. Same root cause. AI can only work with what you give it.

## Run the afternoon audit

You don't need a tool or a budget to start. You need about an hour and a few AI tools. I'd use at least two of these: ChatGPT, Claude, Perplexity or Gemini. Different tools often give different answers, and the differences are part of what you're looking for.

Ask each one these five questions. Replace the brackets with your company name.

1. Describe [company] to someone who has never heard of it. What does it do, who does it serve, and what is it known for? Then tell me where you found this information.
2. Who should hire [company], and why?
3. How does [company] compare to other [your category] companies in [your area]?
4. What do clients or customers say about [company]?
5. What's one thing [company] could be better known for?

As you read the answers, sort what you find into three buckets:

- **What it got right.** This is your foundation. Keep reinforcing it.
- **What it got wrong or outdated.** Old services, former team members, the wrong location, a positioning you moved away from years ago.
- **What it missed completely.** The thing you're proudest of that never came up.

Pay close attention to the sources AI names. That's your map. It shows you exactly which pages are shaping your story.

