## What to fix first

It's tempting to try to fix everything at once. Don't. Start with what you control and what shows up most.

1. **Write your one clear sentence.** What you do, who you serve, and why it matters. If your team can't agree on it, that's your first finding.
2. **Make your own pages say it consistently.** Homepage, about page, service pages, team bios and your LinkedIn company page. Same story, same words.
3. **Update the profiles you've forgotten about.** Directory listings, review sites and old partner pages. These are often the sources AI leans on, and they're the easiest to overlook.
4. **Fill the gaps with real content.** If AI missed what you're proudest of, it's probably because you haven't written about it clearly anywhere. Write the article. Share the story. Answer the question your best clients always ask.
5. **Run the audit again in 90 days.** Not to chase a score. To see whether your story is getting clearer.

None of this is new. It's the same work good marketers have always done: know who you are, say it clearly, and say it everywhere. AI just raised the cost of skipping it.

## The real opportunity

I don't see this as a reason to panic. I see it as a mirror.

AI is reflecting back what the world can find about you. Sometimes that reflection is a pleasant surprise. Sometimes it's a little uncomfortable. Either way, it's useful, because it shows you exactly where your story needs more clarity.

And clarity has always been the work. For your brand, for your team, and for the people trying to decide if you're the right fit.

So run the audit. Then tell me: what did AI get right about you? And what did it get wrong?

